<Base title="For Sources" description="Read this before you send anything.">
  <p class="label">Read this first</p>
  <h2>If you are thinking about sending something</h2>
  <p>
    This is not technical. It takes about five minutes, and it covers the mistakes that actually
    get people identified — which are almost never cryptographic.
  </p>

  <div class="callout vermilion">
    <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
      <strong>Cryptography is not your main risk.</strong> In nearly every case where a source has
      been identified, the failure was ordinary: a work computer, being one of a handful of people
      with access, searching for the journalist's name beforehand, or an invisible watermark in the
      document itself. Aletheia protects the file. You have to protect you.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>Before you do anything</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Not on a work device. Not on a work network.</strong> Employers log more than people
    expect, and the log is the first thing examined. Use a personal device, on a network that is
    not yours and not your employer's.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Do not research this first from your own account.</strong> Looking up this site or "how
    to leak safely" from a traceable account establishes intent and timing before you have started.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Consider Tor.</strong> If the fact that you visited this site at all is dangerous, use
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Think about who else could have this.</strong> If only a handful of people can access
    what you're sending, no technology makes you anonymous by itself. Talk to a journalist about it
    before sending anything.
  </p>

  <h3>About the file itself</h3>
  <p>
    Photographs and video from a modern camera or phone may carry a signature proving they are
    real. That's useful — it's what lets a newsroom trust what you send. It also contains where,
    when, and on what device. Aletheia's drop shows you everything hidden inside your file before
    anything is sent, and lets you choose how coarsely to disclose region and time — the file
    itself never leaves your device.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    This page is a condensed adaptation of <code>docs/for-sources.md</code> in this repository,
    which has more detail on threat models per adversary type.
  </p>
</Base>
